WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.239 --> 00:00:03.643
- I knew that the train
station was somewhere near…

00:00:03.643 --> 00:00:06.310
I am an actor, and I have autism.

00:00:09.335 --> 00:00:11.449
It's really hard for people

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:15.295
with any disability to get a large role

00:00:15.295 --> 00:00:17.462
in a professional theater.

00:00:18.352 --> 00:00:20.015
But for the first time in my life,

00:00:20.015 --> 00:00:24.485
I'm getting to star in a
major professional production.

00:00:24.485 --> 00:00:27.485
(atmospheric music)

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:32.254
- [Actress] Use those words Christopher.

00:00:32.254 --> 00:00:34.602
- You said it on September 12th last year

00:00:34.602 --> 00:00:36.671
at first break.

00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:39.624
My name is Mickey Rowe, and I am playing

00:00:39.624 --> 00:00:42.471
Christopher Boone in the Curious Incident

00:00:42.471 --> 00:00:44.205
of the Dog in the Night-Time.

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:46.558
(enchanting music)

00:00:46.558 --> 00:00:48.901
The Curious Incident of
the Dog in the Night-Time

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.518
is about a boy who has autism

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:56.268
and his adventure that
flips his world upside down.

00:00:56.268 --> 00:00:58.660
- [Actress] I pulled myself
together and I hit him.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.363
- [Mickey] I am the first autistic actor

00:01:01.363 --> 00:01:03.228
to play Christopher Boone,

00:01:03.228 --> 00:01:06.129
and I am surprisingly one of the first

00:01:06.129 --> 00:01:10.296
autistic actors to play any
autistic character anywhere.

00:01:12.013 --> 00:01:13.526
(atmospheric piano music)

00:01:13.526 --> 00:01:15.959
I've had to be an actor my whole life

00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:17.959
to pass as neurotypical.

00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.139
So being an actor comes
so naturally to me.

00:01:23.139 --> 00:01:25.889
I use scripting in my daily life.

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.674
For example, if I went to a coffee shop,

00:01:29.674 --> 00:01:31.201
I might say to the barista,

00:01:31.201 --> 00:01:34.789
“Hi, can I please have
a medium drip coffee.

00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:36.144
“How is your day going today?

00:01:36.144 --> 00:01:37.694
“Has it been busy today?”

00:01:37.694 --> 00:01:39.647
And then regardless of what they say,

00:01:39.647 --> 00:01:41.991
it's been busy or slow, I can then say,

00:01:41.991 --> 00:01:42.966
“Oh, well do you like it better

00:01:42.966 --> 00:01:45.719
“when it's busy or when it's slow?”

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:48.414
So that way I can kind of script my life,

00:01:48.414 --> 00:01:51.112
so I don't have to deal
with social interactions.

00:01:51.112 --> 00:01:51.965
Thank you so much,

00:01:51.965 --> 00:01:54.500
have a good rest of your day.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:55.333
No!

00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:57.683
- Alright son, you keep it.

00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.586
- [Mickey] In rehearsal is my happy spot

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.669
because I have the lines,

00:02:02.604 --> 00:02:04.265
I'm playing a character.

00:02:04.265 --> 00:02:06.287
Everyone else is playing a role,

00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:09.012
be it another character
in the play or director.

00:02:09.012 --> 00:02:09.845
- Alright, we have to stop there

00:02:09.845 --> 00:02:11.367
and take five, you guys.

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:13.491
- During five minute
breaks or 10 minute breaks

00:02:13.491 --> 00:02:18.220
or lunch breaks, is when
you feel less comfortable.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.459
You don't have a specific role

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:23.109
during that time, which makes it confusing

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.396
for someone on the spectrum.

00:02:25.396 --> 00:02:29.520
On stage, there's often
bright lights shining on you,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.016
and loud noises that come out of nowhere

00:02:32.016 --> 00:02:33.848
as sound cues.

00:02:33.848 --> 00:02:36.176
But you've rehearsed all
of these things in advance,

00:02:36.176 --> 00:02:38.440
and you know where each sound cue

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.296
is happening 'cause it happens in the exact

00:02:40.296 --> 00:02:42.128
same place every night.

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:46.393
So it almost feels like you're
in control of those things.

00:02:46.393 --> 00:02:48.934
And because you know what to expect,

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.589
you're in on the magic.

00:02:50.589 --> 00:02:52.286
(enchanting music)

00:02:52.286 --> 00:02:54.165
I would have to talk to
other people, though.

00:02:54.165 --> 00:02:56.817
From mission control, but we would do that

00:02:56.817 --> 00:02:59.561
through a radio link up and a TV monitor,

00:02:59.561 --> 00:03:01.285
so it wouldn't be like talking.

00:03:01.285 --> 00:03:04.124
It's so rare for any
actor with any disability

00:03:04.124 --> 00:03:07.309
to get to play a leading
role in any production,

00:03:07.309 --> 00:03:11.142
let alone a professional
traveling production.

00:03:12.332 --> 00:03:15.972
I get to show people that
people with disabilities,

00:03:15.972 --> 00:03:17.780
we get the job done.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.113
(clapping and cheering)

